// Mapbox build settings for WHOOPsy — THE COMMITTED BASE.
//
// This file is checked in and carries NO secret. It exists so the Xcode project always has a base
// configuration to read: `WhoopsyApp`'s Debug and Release configurations both name it through
// `baseConfigurationReference`, and `xcodebuild` treats a base configuration it cannot open as a
// hard error —
//
//     error: Unable to open base configuration reference file '…/App/Config/Mapbox.xcconfig'
//
// — which fails the whole build before a single source file is compiled. That is why the token is
// NOT in this file. The two lines below are the whole of the arrangement:
//
//     1. `MBX_ACCESS_TOKEN` is set to empty. This is the value a build with no credentials uses.
//     2. `#include?` then pulls in the local override *if it exists*. `?` is the optional include:
//        a missing file is silently skipped rather than being an error, and because an xcconfig
//        assignment is last-one-wins, the include coming last is what lets it override the empty
//        default above it.
//
// So a fresh clone builds, and the built `Info.plist` gets an empty `MBXAccessToken` —
// `MapboxSetup.configure()` returns `false`, `UnavailableOfflineMaps` is handed to the session, the
// `USE OFFLINE MAP` switch is never offered and the route card draws `MapKit`. That is the feature's
// safety property, and it is a property of these two lines.
//
// To turn the feature on, copy `Mapbox.local.xcconfig.example` to `Mapbox.local.xcconfig` in this
// directory and paste your token into it. That file is gitignored — see `.gitignore` — so it stays
// out of the tree. Recipe in `README.md` §Building.
//
// The order matters and must not be tidied: swapping these two lines puts the empty assignment last,
// the included token is discarded, and the app silently reverts to `MapKit` with no build error and
// nothing on screen to say why.

MBX_ACCESS_TOKEN =
#include? "Mapbox.local.xcconfig"
